WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
An industrial control system combines people, procedures, hardware, software,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.440
and networks to monitor and control a physical process.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.355
Field devices measure conditions and perform actions.

00:00:10.355 --> 00:00:11.535
Controllers execute logic.

00:00:11.535 --> 00:00:16.615
HMIs and SCADA platforms give operators visibility and supervisory control.

00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:20.040
Engineering workstations hold privileged configuration and programming capabilities,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.150
while historians preserve process evidence over time.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.380
Industrial networks connect these functions through Ethernet, serial, wireless,

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.650
and specialized protocols whose timing and availability requirements may differ from

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.615
ordinary enterprise traffic.

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:36.360
Architecture models help teams understand the field, control, supervisory, operations,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.780
and enterprise relationships.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.820
The Purdue model is a useful reference,

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.560
and an industrial DMZ can mediate necessary exchange,

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.320
but each organization must document its real systems, data flows, remote paths,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.580
and dependencies.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.230
Security depends on process context.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.215
The same technical event can have different consequences depending on equipment state,

00:00:53.215 --> 00:00:55.650
operational timing, redundancy, and safety design.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:00.160
Cybersecurity teams need operators and engineers to interpret evidence and choose actions

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.315
that do not create additional hazards.

00:01:02.315 --> 00:01:06.005
Strong ICS security protects safety, reliability, availability,

00:01:06.005 --> 00:01:10.355
and process integrity through accurate inventory, segmentation, controlled access,

00:01:10.355 --> 00:01:14.180
trusted configuration, passive monitoring, change management, backups,

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.220
and practiced response.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.280
The central lesson is architectural:

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.640
protect each component according to its operational role and protect the pathways that

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.710
allow those components to work as a system.
